   109	widens with the horizon (+0.09 at T=10, +0.17 at T=200).
   110	
   111	Reasoning effort, the output cap and the three environment gaps were each varied and
   112	none accounts for it (§2, §6). The model is nominally theirs, but §7 records that
   113	`gemini-3-flash-preview` may not be the checkpoint behind their `Gemini-3-Flash`, so we
   114	cannot rule the model out either — only note that we did not change it.
   115	
   116	**Where their ordering breaks.** Memory comes last in our environment; they place it
   117	above ReAct. **This is a difference of ours, not a finding**: our summariser compresses to a
   118	5,770-token mean prompt at T=200 (measured in the Appendix-B environment at the same
   119	output cap; the faithful-environment cells were measured with an instrument that did
   120	not record tokens, §6) where theirs occupies 84,364 — a factor of **14**. Their
   121	"Memory (Summary)" barely summarises; it retains almost as much context as ReAct.
   122	
   123	### The same protocol on a second model
   124	
   125	Claude Haiku 4.5 via Microsoft Foundry, **with the environment and decision cap of the
   126	table above** — faithful environment, output cap 8192 — but not its decoding: Gemini runs
   127	with `temperature=0`, `top_p=1` and reasoning budget 0, while Haiku receives neither
   128	setting (the SDK no longer accepts `temperature`) and runs without extended thinking. At
   129	the two ends of the horizon, 3 seeds × 8
   130	repetitions = **24 runs per cell**, every episode with a per-step trace and a conditions
   131	header. Gemini's figures from the table above in parentheses.
   132	
   133	| T | ReAct | Memory | Stateful | SKILL.state |
   134	|---|---|---|---|---|
   135	| 50 | 0.978 ± 0.045 (0.936) | 0.809 ± 0.184 (0.953) | 0.991 ± 0.026 (0.996) | 0.999 ± 0.004 (1.000) |
   136	| 200 | 0.974 ± 0.070 (0.913) | 0.653 ± 0.157 (0.810) | **0.999 ± 0.002** (0.930) | **0.958 ± 0.058** (1.000) |
   137	
   138	Stateful cells are 15 runs (3 seeds × 5); the rest, 24. Haiku's Stateful needed a third
   139	parser version: the second still dropped the patches Haiku writes in Markdown
   140	(`**StateUpdate:**` followed by a code block) — 731 of 3,000 at T=200, in 7 of 15
   141	episodes — which an adversarial review found (§6, item 7). The cells above use the third,
   142	which applies 2,995 of 3,000 at T=200 and 747 of 750 at T=50. Of the eight it does not
   143	apply, five are valid empty patches (`{}`, nothing to change) and three contain no
   144	object at all.
   145	
   146	ReAct is flat on Haiku: −0.005 between T=50 and T=200, against −0.023 on Gemini and
   147	−0.140 in the paper over the same interval. The full-history arm the paper shows
   148	collapsing degrades on Haiku less than SKILL.state (−0.041); only Stateful degrades less.
   149	
   150	**SKILL.state fails on Haiku, and the adjudication says where.** We replay the
   151	environment with the actions the model executed and compare, step by step, the inventory
   152	the model believes (SKU, units and lot per shelf) against the real one
   153	(`experiments/adjudicar_estado.py`). A failure is attributed to the state only when the
   154	believed inventory prescribes a **different** action than the real one and the model
   155	executed exactly the action the believed inventory prescribes. "The belief matches
   156	reality" means agreement on every field the model wrote; a field it left unspecified —
   157	a shelf recorded only by its SKU, say — is not counted as a discrepancy. Of the 201
   158	failures at T=200:
   159	
   160	- **167 are caused by the state** in that sense; **17** occur while the belief differed
   161	  from reality but the discrepancy does not explain them (both inventories prescribe the
   162	  same action, or the model followed neither); **17** occur with a correct state.
   163	- **In all 17 episodes where the belief departed from reality, the origin is the same:
   164	  a `Move` executed correctly with a state patch written wrong.** `Move` is the only
   165	  transition that requires copying one shelf's contents into another key of the state,
   166	  and that copy is where it breaks — the model writes the contents of a different shelf,
   167	  the lot of the pallet it stored one step earlier, or the wrong number of units.
   168	- **Only in 7 of those 17 did the error reach a decision, all in seed 2.** In 6 of its
   169	  8 runs the corruption happens at the same step (132), after which the model ships from
   170	  a shelf that does not hold the SKU, cascades through ~25 state-caused failures and does
   171	  not correct its state even when the environment rejects the action. In seed 0 the
   172	  corrupted field is a lot number that no later decision reads: 8 corrupted runs, no
   173	  failure caused by it.
   174	
   175	What SKILL.state removes is the need to **reconstruct** the state from history. What it
   176	introduces is the need to **write** it correctly at every transition, and nothing in the
   177	runtime checks that the write matches what the action did. Gemini's belief never departed
   178	from reality in any of its 75 episodes; Haiku wrote at least one wrong patch in 17 of 24
   179	at T=200, and in 7 of them the error reached a decision. The paper's thesis survives in
   180	direction — SKILL.state is still the best arm on Gemini and within 0.02 of ReAct on
   181	Haiku — but "explicit state does not degrade" is a property of the model as much as of
   182	the runtime.
   183	
   184	**Stateful orders the two models in opposite directions.** At T=200 Stateful is the best
   185	arm on Haiku (0.999, above SKILL.state's 0.958) and, on Gemini, is indistinguishable
   186	from ReAct (0.930 against 0.913) and well below SKILL.state (1.000). The same
   187	adjudication describes both — it describes, it does not separate causes, because
   188	Stateful and SKILL.state also differ in response format, parsing, retries and prompt
   189	construction, not only in carrying the history:
   190	
   191	- **On Haiku, Stateful makes the same kind of write error as SKILL.state, far less
   192	  consequentially, and loses nothing to it.** Its belief departs from reality in 8 of 15
   193	  episodes at T=200 — always a correct action with a miswritten patch, the same failure
   194	  as above — but the wrong state prescribes a different action on only 4 steps (against
   195	  168 for SKILL.state), and on all 4 the model does what reality requires. **None** of
   196	  its 2 failures is caused by the state; both happen with a correct one.
   197	- **On Gemini it is the other way round.** Its belief departs from reality in 11 of 15
   198	  episodes at T=200 (5 from a miswritten patch, 6 from a wrong action the state did not
   199	  reflect), where SKILL.state on the same model never did in 75. Of its 211 failures, 86
   200	  are caused by the state, 48 occur with a discrepancy that does not explain them, and
   201	  **77 happen with a correct state in front of it**.
   202	
   203	A hypothesis for why fixing the parser changed so little, which we have not tested: in
   204	Stateful the model's responses go into the history, and each contains its own
   205	`StateUpdate: {...}`. The transcript therefore carried a state written by the model
   206	whether or not the runtime applied it, so the defective arm measured "state kept only in
   207	the transcript" rather than "no state".
   208	
   209	**Memory on Haiku runs into our summariser cap, and a control bounds — but does not
   210	establish — what that costs it.** The summariser has its own output cap (1,200 tokens, with an instruction
   211	to stay under 400 words). Gemini never reaches it — 0 truncated summaries in 30 episodes.
   212	Haiku overruns it constantly: 194 truncated summaries in the 24 episodes at T=50 and
   213	**2,386 at T=200**, about half of all summaries. A control arm at T=50 with the cap
   214	raised to 4,096 (3 seeds × 3, `--summary-max-tokens`) truncates nothing and scores
   215	**0.889 ± 0.126 (n=9) against 0.809 ± 0.184 (n=24)**: +0.080, Welch 95 % interval
   216	−0.037 to +0.197. The control removes the truncation and raises the observed mean, but
   217	does not establish an effect on the score, and raising the cap also changes how long a
   218	summary may be, not only whether the same summary is cut. Uncut, Memory still stays well
   219	below ReAct (0.978). Haiku's Memory column is therefore read as *our summariser with a cap one
   220	model respects and the other does not*, and we draw no cross-model conclusion from it.
   221	
   222	### Dense noise (their Experiment 2)
   223	
   224	T=50 fixed, distractors appended to the observation under their header, 15 runs per
   225	level:
   226	
   227	| Noise (events/turn) | Our ReAct | Theirs |
   228	|---|---|---|
   229	| 0 | 0.961 ± 0.037 | 0.88 |
   230	| 5 | 0.952 ± 0.066 | 0.68 |
   231	| 20 | 0.959 ± 0.056 | 0.61 |
   232	| 50 | 0.859 ± 0.121 | 0.53 |
   233	
   234	From 0 to 50 events: **−0.103, 3.1 standard errors**. Theirs: −0.350. **The effect is real, and what we can
   235	resolve is the extreme**: 0, 5 and 20 are not distinguishable at this sample size — their
   236	means span 0.009 with standard deviations near 0.06 — so the measured effect is carried
   237	by the jump to 50. A gradual decline of a few points across the lower levels would be
   238	invisible here.
   239	And what changes most is not the mean but the dispersion, which triples: under 50
   240	distractors the model is not stably worse — it is **erratic**.
   241	
   242	---
   243	
   244	## 4. Cost accounting with caching
   245	
   246	The paper compares **tokens**; whoever pays the bill compares **money**. An append-only
   247	transcript caches almost entirely; a mutating state block invalidates the prefix.
   248	
   249	Measured on Claude Haiku 4.5 via Microsoft Foundry (T=50, 3 seeds, caching on,
   250	short procedure):
   251	
   252	| Runtime | Score | Raw tokens | Effective cost | Cache saving |
   253	|---|---|---|---|---|
   254	| ReAct | 1.00 | 826k | **152k** | **82 %** |
   255	| Stateful | 1.00 | 873k | 873k | 0 % |
   256	| Memory | 0.87 | 313k | 313k | 0 % |
   257	| SKILL.state | 1.00 | **109k** | 109k | 0 % |
   258	
   259	**SKILL.state's advantage over ReAct goes from 7.54x in raw input tokens to 1.39x in
   260	effective input**, and the ordering inverts: by tokens SKILL.state < Memory < ReAct <
   261	Stateful; by effective input SKILL.state < **ReAct** < Memory < Stateful.
   262	
   263	Two bounds on that number, both material:
   264	
   265	- **These are input tokens, not a full invoice.** Output is not priced in. Mean output
   266	  per episode is 5,740 tokens for ReAct and 9,674 for SKILL.state — neither zero nor
   267	  equal, and they move the comparison in SKILL.state's disfavour.
   268	- **It depends on the length of the static prefix.** With the long procedure (~4,900
   269	  tokens, above the cacheable minimum), the arms that compress start caching too:
   270	  SKILL.state saves 79 %, Memory 48 %, Stateful 22 %, and SKILL.state's advantage is
   271	  2.51x rather than 1.39x. The conflict below is therefore **conditional on a short
   272	  static prefix**, not a property of compression as such.
   273	
   274	Three consequences:
   275	
   276	1. **With a short static prefix, compressing and caching are in conflict.** Every
   277	   method that compresses rewrites the prefix, and rewriting the prefix costs the
   278	   cache. With a long static prefix the conflict softens: the fixed block caches even
   279	   for the compressing arms.
   280	2. **ReAct and Stateful send nearly the same content and are billed 5.7x apart.**
   281	   ReAct saves 82 %, Stateful 0 %. This contrast does **not** isolate prompt order: the
   282	   two runners also differ in whether the history is marked as a cacheable prefix (only
   283	   ReAct marks it), and the Stateful figure was measured with the defective parser of §6
   284	   (item 7), whose state block was empty and therefore constant. Order, cache marking
   285	   and a mutating block are confounded here. **Isolated, the effect of order is 5.2x.**
   286	   Stateful on Haiku at T=50, 3 seeds × 2 runs per arm, both arms marking the history
   287	   as a cacheable prefix and sending the same content (~880k raw input tokens per
   288	   episode); only the position of the state block changes. State first: 74 % of the
   289	   input is cache *writes* and 22 % reads, an effective 869k per episode, a 1 % saving.
   290	   History first: 91 % reads and 4 % writes, an effective 168k, an 81 % saving. Scores do
   291	   not differ (0.997 and 1.000). One of the six state-first runs never applied a patch —
   292	   it wrote shelf numbers as unquoted keys, which is not JSON — so its state block stayed
   293	   constant and cached like the other arm (160k effective); excluding it, the ratio is
   294	   6.0x. We report 5.2x because it is what the arm as specified cost, and 6.0x as the
   295	   effect among runs whose state actually changed. Their Appendix A.3 template puts the state block first.
   296	3. **It reconciles an anomaly in their table.** Their totals column sits ~3.5x below
   297	   horizon × mean prompt at every horizon. With caching it fits: their totals would be
   298	   billed, their mean prompt raw.
   299	
   300	### The magnitude does not transfer across providers
   301	
   302	Measured on Vertex with `gemini-3-flash-preview`, **within a single grid** (original
   303	environment, output cap 600, reasoning budget 0, n=5 episodes per cell) so that the
   304	comparison is not mixing conditions. The figure is cache-read tokens over total input
   305	tokens:
   306	
   307	| Runtime | T=25 | T=50 | T=100 | T=200 |
   308	|---|---|---|---|---|
   309	| ReAct | 1.1 % | 4.6 % | 6.8 % | **7.0 %** |
   310	| Stateful | 0 % | 2.6 % | 4.3 % | 4.3 % |

